 In beta plus decay, a proton in the nucleus converts into a neutron, emitting a positron ($\beta^+$) and an electron neutrino ($\nu_e$). The mass of a proton is approximately 1.007276 amu, and the mass of a neutron is approximately 1.008665 amu. The mass of an electron is approximately 0.00054858 amu.

The mass difference between the parent nucleus (N-13) and the daughter nucleus (C-13) plus the positron is equal to the kinetic energy of the positron.
Let $m_p$ be the mass of a proton, $m_n$ be the mass of a neutron, and $m_e$ be the mass of an electron.
The mass of N-13 is $13.00335 u$.
The mass of C-13 is $12.99876 u$.
The mass of a positron is $m_e \approx 0.00054858 u$.

